{
  "metadata": {
    "evaluation_name": "realism_check_and_graded_comfort_metric",
    "description": "Realism calibration of the synthetic occupant-trajectory generator against real CASAS households; corrected/reproduced bootstrap CIs plus a formal power analysis on the n=3 energy-savings result; a graded (non-saturated) comfort proxy bypassing MissTime's reactive-fallback floor; and the full 3x4x2 ROC-AUC table with an omnibus paired significance test.",
    "narrative_interpretation": "(1) Realism: synthetic topology closest to real CASAS behavior is 'house_5room_irregular' (z-distance to tulum = 1.82); farthest is 'studio_linear_regular' (z-distance to tulum = 4.13). (2) The n=3 cross-topology bootstrap CIs reproduce the original artifact's CIs (with some drift); power analysis shows the current n=3 design cannot detect anything smaller than MDE~155.9pp at FPR=0.05, and would need n~797 independent topology/household conditions to reliably detect a 5pp mean savings effect at 80% power -- the 'CI includes zero' result is better read as inconclusive/underpowered than as a confirmed null. (3) The graded comfort proxy, which bypasses PreHeat's reactive-fallback floor that saturates binary MissTime, finds a pooled integrated-temperature-deficit difference (transition-PreHeat) of -53184.0 degC*min (bootstrap 95% CI [-150880.4509169076, -3326.4720407114837], excludes zero) and a pooled realized-lead-time difference of -16.7 min (CI [-44.73960944441972, -2.00935221257151], excludes zero), directly probing whether the transition model's forecasting edge produces any real anticipatory comfort benefit invisible to MissTime. (4) The full 3-topology x 4-lookahead AUC table (12 cells) shows the transition predictor dominates PreHeat's ROC-AUC in 12/12 cells (mean gap +0.115, paired bootstrap 95% CI [0.10657802038797787, 0.12271695512745037], Wilcoxon one-sided p=2.44e-04), giving a single omnibus statistical statement for the prediction-stage dominance claim across every topology and lookahead, not just the 2-of-3 previously narrated. NOTE: the graded comfort proxy surfaces a mechanism previously invisible to MissTime -- at every matched-FPR operating point, method.py's own threshold_at_fpr() (reused verbatim, not re-derived) resolves the transition-predictor's crossing threshold to 1.0, so predicted_occupied is essentially never True for the transition model and its anticipatory-heating branch is almost never taken; heating for the transition condition in the original simulation is therefore driven almost entirely by the shared reactive-fallback rule, not by its (superior) forecasts. This is a plausible root cause for the disconnect between the strong AUC advantage and the null energy-savings result, and for the negative pooled comfort-proxy diffs above.",
